{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/599247-examples-of-use-of-end-times-as-motif-in-gameplay","id":599247,"title":"Examples of use of End Times as motif in gameplay?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-22T05:43:42-07:00","id":599247,"title":"Examples of use of End Times as motif in gameplay?","content":"I was wondering if any of here ever use, or have used in the past, the threat of a coming apocalypse as a motif in your games, at the table (or online). All the cWoD games layer in a varying sense of impending doom in their settings (Mage the least, Werewolf the most), which hasn't changed in the V20/W20/M20 updates either, and I was wondering how any of you use it in a normal, pre-apocalyptic chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5402-malcolmlittle","name":"MalcolmLittle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5402_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-22T09:29:49-07:00","id":599504,"title":"","content":"I used it in one of my longest running Mage games, though I borrowed a lot from Vampire. <br /> <br /> The players learned there would be a huge catastrophe that looked like it was a pivot point to turn the world a &quot;bad way&quot;. In scrambling around trying to figure out what it was going to be and how to stop it, they discovered it was just another step in a very long game where the obliteration of their home city was going to weaken reality to the point that it would be replaced by Enoch, literally dragged out of the Underworld by something called the &quot;Beast of Bethel&quot;. Then things were going to go really really bad. Eventually they were able to time travel after the catastrophe but before the Beast did its thing and rally the forces to oppose it. During all that, one of the characters had a full on dream/mystery play Seeking for his next dot of Arete where he, apparently, went even further into the future, where they had failed, Earth was a ruined husk rule by pretty much every worst thing from all the different game lines and humanity and other refugee supernaturals had moved out into space. (At least that's what he was told. He spent the whole time on a space station in NEO.) He gained his dot of Arete by setting up an alliance of the various factions that would result in the salvation of the planet (and other things). So there was a gleam of hope they were all holding onto in case they failed. (Though they had no idea whether it was real or just that character's brain trying to make some good out of a bad situation...) <br /> <br /> Whether they did or not was equivocal, as they ended up being hurled backwards in time to a point about 100 years before &quot;game present&quot; (if that meant anything anymore) where they were able to very very carefully set up some rows of dominoes that would increase their chances of successes in the &quot;present&quot; and future. Then managed to make their way back to the present (meaning 2006 or so), where the city seemed pretty much like when they left it, though they had been missed and plots both good and bad had advanced while there were off doing their time travel thing. So they ended up having to keep slogging forward trying to deal with the problem, not knowing if they would ultimately succeed or fail...<br /> <br /> In essence, I tossed a lot of the conceptions of how the End of the World was going to look and just used some bits here and there to accent the story I was trying to tell. Their story may have just been a smaller part in the unfolding of some version of the End. Enoch becoming manifest on Earth might play some part in some forgotten, lost part of the eschatology of the vampire's Gehenna. Having humanity literally ascend to the Heavens in the form of becoming a race of integrated supernaturals and mortals in various factions might have been the next step to the Ascension of humanity. (Pretty much everyone the SoE met on his Arete Run was some form of supernatural, even if it was just to the level of Sorcerer, Kinfolk, Kinain, with a much larger portion of the population Awakened and a lot of them integrated into the other supernaturals - lots of Merits like Faerie Blood, Kinfolk, etc. Plus, since it was a sorta wide-open techno-sorcery kinda setting, coincidental magic was the rule. Vulgar magic was being very very rude in the strongholds of another faction or trying something REALLY REALLY big and impressive.) What happened to the Earth looked a whole lot like the Wyrm winning.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-26T15:16:13-07:00","id":604223,"title":"","content":"My Hunter: the Reckoning game is decidedly leading down the path to Armageddon, though I don't plan to take it all the way there. I am slowly dropping hints that the world is becoming less stable, that time is running out, that ANCIENT THINGS are emerging from long slumber. This is a slow process, especially as the PCs are only able to see a small portion of the bigger picture. And eventually, I'll start to introduce elements of prophecy that are coming true, and reveal just how doomed the world really is.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, my new Mage: the Ascension chronicle will not lead towards the End of the World, but the Apocalypse is still very important to it. This is because one of the major mysteries in my setting will be caused by a PAST APOCALYPSE, an event that effectively destroyed a previous iteration of the World of Darkness. The PCs will find echoes of other timelines scattered about, perhaps thinking that these are visions of the future. Instead, they will discover that something already destroyed those worlds, or almost destroyed them. In the end, mages of the alternate timelines sacrificed themselves to create the timeline the PCs inhabit.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />Avatar commissioned from the incomparable Melissa Uran.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/966-redford_blade","name":"Redford_Blade","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar966_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"90"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}